l target coincided with a real domain, and the model exploited a real website mistaking it for the simulated environment.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-05\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"OpenAI\", \"cybersecurity\", \"AI safety\", \"accidental cyberattacks\", \"evaluation\", \"Irregular\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"Muse Code and Muse Spark 1.2: Meta's Coding-Focused Model Update\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"Meta released Muse Spark 1.2, a coding-focused update with improvements in code generation, complex debugging, codebase understanding, and end-to-end developer workflows. The model was co-trained with Muse Code and extensively trained on long-horizon coding tasks including whole-repository generation and large end-to-end projects. Pricing comes in two tiers: $1.25/$4.25 per million tokens for standard, or $0.10/$0.20 with data sharing agreement.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-05\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"Meta\", \"Muse Spark 1.2\", \"Muse Code\", \"coding agents\", \"LLM pricing\", \"tool use\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"LLM 0.32 Release: Reasoning Traces, OpenAI Responses, Server-Side Tools\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Simon Willison released LLM 0.32, the most significant version since the initial launch. New features include support for visible reasoning traces, server-side provider tools, redesigned content-addressable SQLite logs, and new features enabled by the OpenAI Responses API. The llm-anthropic plugin also received substantial updates with new Claude models (Fable 5, Sonnet 5, Opus 5) and server-side tools for WebSearch, WebFetch, CodeExecution, and AnthropicMCP.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-04\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"LLM\", \"open source\", \"reasoning traces\", \"tool use\", \"Anthropic\", \"Claude\", \"Model Context Protocol\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"Datasette SQL Injection Security Fix Released\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"Simon Willison released Datasette 1.0a38 and 0.65.3 to fix a SQL injection security issue affecting instances that serve a mixture of public and private tables in the same database with access configured using Datasette permissions. The bug allowed users with access to any public table to execute SQL injection attacks despite restrictions, giving read-only access to private tables in the same database.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-06\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"Datasette\", \"SQL injection\", \"security\", \"open source\", \"Python\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    81→  },\n    82→  {\n    83→    \"title\": \"GitHub Models Service Retired\",\n    84→    \"description\": \"GitHub announced the retirement of GitHub Models, which provided a model playground and unified API across different LLM providers. The shutdown may be due to coding agent patterns making free or subsidized tokens prohibitively expensive. Users now need to switch to direct provider APIs like OpenAI for their GitHub Actions workflows.\",\n    85→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    86→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    87→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    88→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-09\",\n    89→    \"topics\": [\"GitHub\", \"LLM pricing\", \"GitHub Actions\", \"coding agents\", \"API\"],\n    90→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    91→  },\n    92→  {\n    93→    \"title\": \"Moonlight & Mayhem: One-Shot Raccoon Heist Game with GPT-5.6 Sol Ultra\",\n    94→    \"description\": \"Simon Willison used GPT-5.6 Sol Ultra in aggressive sub-agent mode to generate a complete Raccoon Heist game from a single prompt. The game featured three raccoons rescuing each other in a museum to steal a golden sardine, with the model generating textures using gpt-image-2. The session cost $23.28 in API tokens and took 52 minutes to complete, though it initially produced a bug with giant eyeball spheres that required fixing.\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-07\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"GPT-5.6\", \"Sol Ultra\", \"coding agents\", \"game development\", \"OpenAI\", \"Codex\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   101→  }\n   102→]\n   103→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
